e>Basinski</surname><given-names>AS</given-names></name><name><surname>Blackstien-Hirsch</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Naylor</surname><given-names>CD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The need for acute, subacute and nonacute care at 105 general hospital sites in Ontario. Joint Policy and Planning Committee Non-Acute Hospitalization Project Working Group</article-title><source>Canadian Medical Association Journal</source><year>1998</year><volume>158</volume><fpage>1289</fpage><lpage>1296</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9614821</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Poulos</surname><given-names>CJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Eagar</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Determining appropriateness for rehabilitation or other subacute care: is there a role for utilisation review?</article-title><source>Australia and New Zealand Health Policy</source><year>2007</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>3</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17352832</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1743-8462-4-3</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>General practitioner appointment systems, patient satisfaction, and use of accident and emergency services &#x02013; a study in one geographical area</article-title><source>Family Practice</source><year>1994</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>438</fpage><lpage>445</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7895974</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/fampra/11.4.438</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Richards</surname><given-names>SH</given-names></name><name><surname>Pound</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Dickens</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Greco</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Campbell</surname><given-names>JL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Exploring users' experiences of accessing out-of-hours primary medical care services</article-title><source>Quality &#x00026; Safety in Health Care</source><year>2007</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>469</fpage><lpage>477</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18055893</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1136/qshc.2006.021501</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dalsgaard</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Kallerup</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Rosendal</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Outreach visits to improve dementia care in general practice: a qualitative study</article-title><source>International Journal for Quality in Health Care</source><year>2007</year><volume>19</volume><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>273</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17693445</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/intqhc/mzm033</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Boulet</surname><given-names>LP</given-names></name><name><surname>Borduas</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Bouchard</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Blais</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Hargreave</surname><given-names>FE</given-names></name><name><surname>Rouleau</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Playing cards on asthma management: a new interactive method for knowledge transfer to primary care physicians</article-title><source>Can Respir J</source><year>2007</year><volume>14</volume><fpage>480</fpage><lpage>484</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18060093</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Watkins</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Timm</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Gooberman-Hill</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Harvey</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Haines</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Donovan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Factors affecting feasibility and acceptability of a practice-based educational intervention to support evidence-based prescribing: a qualitative study</article-title><source>Family Practice</source><year>2004</year><volume>21</volume><fpage>661</fpage><lpage>669</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15528289</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/fampra/cmh614</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Stewart</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name><name><surname>Shamdasani</surname><given-names>PN</given-names></name><name><surname>Rook</surname><given-names>DW</given-names></name></person-group><source>Focus groups: theory and practice</source><year>2007</year><volume>20</volume><edition>2</edition><publisher-name>London: Sage Publications</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>